You’re right—I was too categorical and used extreme, instantaneous examples as though they described normal field behavior.

In practice, a cloud passing over a PV system does **not necessarily cause a dramatic “plummet.”** The observed response depends on:

- cloud optical thickness and whether it covers the whole array;
- the array’s size, layout, and orientation;
- cloud movement relative to the site;
- whether you are measuring plane-of-array irradiance, DC power, AC output, or grid-level net output;
- inverter clipping, control response, and the plant’s operating point.

A large plant can remain partly illuminated while a cloud crosses it, so its aggregate output may show a gradual ramp or only a moderate reduction. Even at a small site, many clouds are sufficiently thin or broken that diffuse irradiance maintains much of the output. The large reductions I mentioned are possible under a **dense, effectively opaque cloud covering the relevant array**, but they should not have been presented as typical.

The technically sound statement is simply:

> Clouds make PV output variable and can produce ramps, but the size and speed of the change are highly site- and cloud-dependent; a cloud passing overhead does not automatically mean solar output plummets.

My earlier numerical ranges were not appropriate without specifying the measurement and scenario.

